\mt1 1 Corinthians
\c 1
\s1 Greetings from Paul and Sosthenes
\r (\ref Acts 18:1–11|ACT 18:1-11\ref*; \ref 2 Corinthians 1:1–2|2CO 1:1-2\ref*)
\p \v 1 Paul, called \add to be\add* an apostle of Christ Jesus by \add the\add* will of God, and \add our\add* brother Sosthenes,
\p \v 2 To the church of God in Corinth, \add to those\add* sanctified in Christ Jesus \add and\add* called \add to be\add* holy, together with all those everywhere \add who\add* call on the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, their \add Lord\add* and ours:
\p \v 3 Grace and peace to you from God our Father and \add the\add* Lord Jesus Christ.
\s1 Thanksgiving
\r (\ref Philippians 1:3–11|PHP 1:3-11\ref*; \ref Colossians 1:3–14|COL 1:3-14\ref*)
\p \v 4 I always thank my God for you because of the grace \add He\add* has given you in Christ Jesus. \v 5 For in Him you have been enriched in every way, in all speech and all knowledge, \v 6 because \add our\add* testimony about Christ was confirmed in you.
\p \v 7 Therefore you do not lack \add any\add* spiritual gift as you eagerly await the revelation of our Lord Jesus Christ. \v 8 \add He\add* will sustain you to \add the\add* end, \add so that you will be\add* blameless on the day of our Lord Jesus Christ. \v 9 God, who has called you into fellowship with His Son Jesus Christ our Lord, \add is\add* faithful.
\s1 Unity in the Church
\r (\ref Psalm 133:1–3|PSA 133:1-3\ref*; \ref Ephesians 4:1–16|EPH 4:1-16\ref*)
\p \v 10 I appeal to you, brothers, in the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, that all of you agree together, \add so that\add* there may be no divisions among you and \add that\add* you may be united in mind and conviction. \v 11 My brothers, some from Chloe’s household have informed me that there are quarrels among you. \v 12 What I mean \add is\add* this: Individuals among you are saying, “I \add follow\add* Paul,” “I \add follow\add* Apollos,” “I \add follow\add* Cephas,”\f + \fr 1:12 \ft That is, Peter\f* \add or\add* “I \add follow\add* Christ.”
\p \v 13 \add Is\add* Christ divided? \add Was\add* Paul crucified for you? Were you baptized into the name of Paul? \v 14 I thank God that I did not baptize any of you except Crispus and Gaius, \v 15 so no one can say that you were baptized into my name. \v 16 Yes, I also baptized the household of Stephanas; beyond that I do not remember if I baptized anyone else. \v 17 For Christ \add did\add* not send me to baptize, but to preach the gospel, not with words of wisdom, lest the cross of Christ be emptied of \add its\add* power.
\s1 The Message of the Cross
\p \v 18 For the message of the cross is foolishness to those who are perishing, but to us who are being saved it is \add the\add* power of God. \v 19 For it is written:
\b 
\q1 “I will destroy the wisdom of the wise;
\q2 the intelligence of the intelligent I will frustrate.”\f + \fr 1:19 \ref Isaiah 29:14|ISA 29:14\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 20 Where \add is the\add* wise \add man\add*? Where \add is the\add* scribe? Where \add is the\add* philosopher of this age? \add Has\add* not God made foolish the wisdom of the world? \v 21 For since in the wisdom of God the world through \add its\add* wisdom \add did\add* not know \add Him\add*, God was pleased through the foolishness of what was preached to save those who believe.
\p \v 22 Jews demand signs and Greeks search for wisdom, \v 23 but we preach Christ crucified, a stumbling block to Jews and foolishness to Gentiles,\f + \fr 1:23 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa to Greeks\f* \v 24 but to those who are called, both Jews and Greeks, Christ \add the\add* power of God and \add the\add* wisdom of God.
\p \v 25 For the foolishness of God is wiser than man’s \add wisdom\add*,\f + \fr 1:25 \ft Literally \fqa than men\ft ; twice in this verse\f* and the weakness of God \add is\add* stronger than man’s \add strength\add*.
\s1 Wisdom from God
\p \v 26 Brothers, consider  \add the time of \add* your calling: Not many \add of you were\add* wise by human standards; not many \add were\add* powerful; not many \add were\add* of noble birth. \v 27 But God chose the foolish things of the world to shame the wise; God chose the weak things of the world to shame the strong. \v 28 \add He\add* chose the lowly and despised things of the world, and the things that are not, to nullify the things that are, \v 29 so that no \add one\add* may boast in \add His\add* presence.
\p \v 30 \add It is\add* because of Him \add that\add* you are in Christ Jesus, who has become for us wisdom from God: \add our\add* righteousness, holiness, and redemption. \v 31 Therefore, as it is written: “\add Let\add* him who boasts boast in \add the\add* Lord.”\f + \fr 1:31 \ref Jeremiah 9:24|JER 9:24\ref*\f*
\c 2
\s1 Paul’s Message by the Spirit’s Power
\p \v 1 \add When\add* I came to you, brothers, I did not come with eloquence or wisdom \add as\add* I proclaimed to you the testimony about God. \v 2 For I resolved to know nothing \add while I was\add* with you except Jesus Christ and Him crucified. \v 3 I came to you in weakness and fear, and with much trembling. \v 4 My message and my preaching \add were\add* not with persuasive words of wisdom, but with a demonstration of \add the\add* Spirit’s power, \v 5 so that your faith would not rest on men’s wisdom, but on God’s power.
\s1 Spiritual Wisdom
\r (\ref Ephesians 1:15–23|EPH 1:15-23\ref*)
\p \v 6 Among the mature, however, we speak a message of wisdom — but not \add the\add* wisdom of this age \add or\add* of the rulers of this age, who are coming to nothing. \v 7 No, we speak of \add the\add* mysterious \add and\add* hidden wisdom of God,\f + \fr 2:7 \ft Or \fqa we speak God’s wisdom in a mystery\f* which \add He\add* destined for our glory before time \add began\add*. \v 8 None of the rulers of this age understood \add it\add*. For if they had, they would not have crucified the Lord of glory. \v 9 Rather, as it is written:
\b 
\q1 “No eye has seen,
\q2 no ear has heard,
\q2 no heart has imagined,
\q1 what God has prepared for those who love Him.”\f + \fr 2:9 \ref Isaiah 64:4|ISA 64:4\ref*\f*
\p \v 10 But God has revealed \add it\add* to us by the Spirit.
\p The Spirit searches all things, even the deep \add things \add* of God. \v 11 For who among men knows the \add thoughts\add* of man except \add his own\add* spirit within him? So too, no one knows the \add thoughts\add* of God except the Spirit of God. \v 12 We have not received the spirit of the world, but the Spirit who \add is\add* from God, that we may understand what God has freely given us. \v 13 And \add this is what\add* we speak, not in words taught \add us\add* by human wisdom, but in \add words\add* taught by \add the\add* Spirit, expressing spiritual truths in spiritual \add words\add*.\f + \fr 2:13 \ft Or \fqa to spiritual people\f*
\p \v 14 \add The\add* natural man \add does\add* not accept the things that come from the Spirit of God. For they are foolishness to him, and he cannot understand \add them\add*, because \add they are\add* spiritually discerned. \v 15 The spiritual \add man\add* judges all things, but he himself is not subject to anyone’s judgment. \v 16 “For who has known \add the\add* mind of \add the\add* Lord, \add so as\add* to instruct Him?”\f + \fr 2:16 \ref Isaiah 40:13|ISA 40:13\ref* (see also LXX)\f* But we have \add the\add* mind of Christ.
\c 3
\s1 God’s Fellow Workers
\r (\ref Hebrews 5:11–14|HEB 5:11-14\ref*)
\p \v 1 Brothers, I could not address you as spiritual, but as worldly — as infants in Christ. \v 2 I gave you milk, not solid food, for you were not yet ready \add for solid food\add*. In fact, you are still not ready, \v 3 for you are still worldly. For since \add there is\add* jealousy and dissension among you, are you not worldly? \add Are you not \add* walking in the way of man? \v 4 For when one \add of you\add* says, “I \add follow\add* Paul,” and another, “I \add follow\add* Apollos,” are you not mere men?
\p \v 5 What then is Apollos? And what is Paul? \add They are\add* servants through whom you believed, as the Lord has assigned to each \add his role \add*. \v 6 I planted \add the seed and\add* Apollos watered \add it\add*, but God made \add it \add* grow. \v 7 So neither he who plants nor he who waters is anything, but \add only\add* God, who makes \add things\add* grow. \v 8 He who plants and he who waters are one  \add in purpose \add*,\f + \fr 3:8 \ft Literally \fqa are one\f* and each will be rewarded according to \add his\add* own labor. \v 9 For we are God’s fellow workers; you are God’s field, God’s building.
\s1 Christ Our Foundation
\r (\ref Isaiah 28:14–22|ISA 28:14-22\ref*; \ref Ephesians 2:19–22|EPH 2:19-22\ref*; \ref 1 Peter 2:1–8|1PE 2:1-8\ref*)
\p \v 10 By the grace God has given me, I laid \add a\add* foundation as an expert builder, and someone else is building on \add it\add*. But each one must be careful how he builds. \v 11 For no one can lay a foundation other than the \add one\add* already laid, which is Jesus Christ.
\p \v 12 If anyone builds on \add this\add* foundation \add using\add* gold, silver, precious stones, wood, hay, \add or\add* straw, \v 13 his workmanship will be evident, because the Day will bring it to light. It will be revealed with fire, and the fire will prove the quality of each \add man’s\add* work. \v 14 If what he has built survives, he will receive a reward. \v 15 If \add it\add* is burned up, he will suffer loss. He himself will be saved, but only as if through \add the\add* flames.
\s1 God’s Temple and God’s Wisdom
\r (\ref Romans 12:1–8|ROM 12:1-8\ref*; \ref 1 Corinthians 6:18–20|1CO 6:18-20\ref*)
\p \v 16 Do you not know that you yourselves are God’s temple, and that God’s Spirit dwells in\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Or \fqa among\f* you? \v 17 If anyone destroys God’s temple, God will destroy him; for God’s temple is holy, \add and\add* you are \add that temple\add*.
\p \v 18 \add Let\add* no one deceive himself. If any of you thinks he is wise in this age, he should become a fool, so that he may become wise. \v 19 For the wisdom of this world is foolishness in God’s sight. \add As\add* it is written: “He catches the wise in their craftiness.”\f + \fr 3:19 \ref Job 5:13|JOB 5:13\ref*\f* \v 20 And again, “\add The\add* Lord knows that the thoughts of the wise are futile.”\f + \fr 3:20 \ref Psalm 94:11|PSA 94:11\ref*\f*
\p \v 21 Therefore, stop boasting in men. All things are yours, \v 22 whether Paul or Apollos or Cephas\f + \fr 3:22 \ft That is, Peter\f* or \add the\add* world or life or death or the present or the future. All \add of them belong to\add* you, \v 23 and you \add belong to\add* Christ, and Christ \add belongs to\add* God.
\c 4
\s1 Servants of Christ
\p \v 1 So then, men ought to regard us as servants of Christ and stewards of the mysteries of God. \v 2 Now it is required of stewards that they be found faithful.
\p \v 3 I care very little, however, if I am judged by you or by any human court. In fact, I do not even judge myself. \v 4 My conscience is clear, but that does not vindicate me. It is \add the\add* Lord who judges me.
\p \v 5 Therefore judge nothing before \add the\add* appointed time; \add wait\add* until the Lord comes. \add He\add* will bring to light what is hidden in darkness and will expose the motives of \add men’s \add* hearts. At that time each will receive \add his\add* praise from God.
\p \v 6 Brothers, I have applied these things to myself and Apollos for your benefit, so that you may learn from us not \add to go\add* beyond what is written. Then you will not take pride in one \add man\add* over another. \v 7 For who makes you so superior? What do you have that you did not receive? And if you did receive \add it\add*, why do you boast as though \add you did not\add*?
\p \v 8 Already you have all you want. Already you have become rich. Without us, you have become kings. How I wish you really were kings, so that we might be kings with you! \v 9 For it seems to me that God has displayed us apostles at the end  \add of the procession \add*, like \add prisoners \add* appointed for death. We have become a spectacle to the whole world, to angels as well as to men.
\p \v 10 We \add are\add* fools for Christ, but you \add are\add* wise in Christ. We \add are\add* weak, but you \add are\add* strong. You \add are\add* honored, but we \add are\add* dishonored. \v 11 To \add this\add* very hour we are hungry and thirsty, we are poorly clothed, we are brutally treated, we are homeless. \v 12 We work hard with \add our\add* own hands. When we are vilified, we bless; when we are persecuted, we endure \add it\add*; \v 13 when we are slandered, we answer gently. Up to this moment we have become \add the\add* scum of the earth, \add the\add* refuse of \add the world\add*.
\s1 Paul’s Fatherly Warning
\p \v 14 I am not writing this to shame you, but to warn you as my beloved children. \v 15 Even if you have ten thousand guardians in Christ, \add you do\add* not \add have\add* many fathers; for in Christ Jesus I became your father through the gospel. \v 16 Therefore I urge you to imitate me. \v 17 That is why I have sent you Timothy, my beloved and faithful child in \add the\add* Lord. \add He\add* will remind you of my way of life in Christ Jesus,\f + \fr 4:17 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa my way of life in Christ,\f* which is exactly what I teach everywhere in every church.
\p \v 18 Some \add of you\add* have become arrogant, as if I were not coming to you. \v 19 But I will come to you shortly, if the Lord is willing, and \add then\add* I will find out not \add only\add* what these arrogant \add people\add* are saying, but what power \add they have\add*. \v 20 For the kingdom of God \add is\add* not a matter of talk but of power. \v 21 Which do you prefer? Shall I come to you with a rod, or in love and \add with\add* a gentle spirit?
\c 5
\s1 Immorality Rebuked
\r (\ref Leviticus 20:10–21|LEV 20:10-21\ref*; \ref Proverbs 5:1–23|PRO 5:1-23\ref*)
\p \v 1 \add It is\add* actually reported \add that there is\add* sexual immorality among you, and \add of a kind\add* that is intolerable even among pagans: A man has \add his\add* father’s wife. \v 2 And you are proud! Shouldn’t you rather have been stricken with grief \add and\add* have removed from your \add fellowship\add* the \add man who\add* did this?
\p \v 3 Although I am absent  \add from you \add* in body, I am present \add with you \add* in spirit, and I have already pronounced judgment on the \add one who\add* did this, just as if I were present. \v 4 \add When\add* you are assembled in the name of our Lord Jesus\f + \fr 5:4 \ft Or \fqa In the name of our Lord Jesus, when you are assembled\f* and I \add am with you\add* in spirit, along with the power of the Lord Jesus, \v 5 hand this man \add over\add* to Satan for \add the\add* destruction of the flesh, so that \add his\add* spirit may be saved on the Day of the Lord.\f + \fr 5:5 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa the Lord Jesus\f*
\p \v 6 Your boasting \add is\add* not good. Do you not know that a little leaven works through the whole batch \add of dough\add*? \v 7 Get rid of the old leaven, that you may be a new unleavened batch, as you really are. For Christ, our Passover lamb, has been sacrificed. \v 8 Therefore let us keep the feast, not with \add the\add* old bread, leavened with malice and wickedness, but with \add the\add* unleavened \add bread\add* of sincerity and of truth.
\s1 Expel the Immoral Brother
\p \v 9 I wrote you in \add my\add* letter not to associate with sexually immoral \add people\add*. \v 10 I was not \add including \add* the sexually immoral of this world, or the greedy and swindlers, or idolaters. In that case you would have to leave \add this\add* world. \v 11 But now I am writing you not to associate with anyone who claims to be a brother \add but\add* is sexually immoral or greedy, an idolater or a verbal abuser, a drunkard or a swindler. With such a man \add do\add* not even eat.
\p \v 12 What business of mine \add is it\add* to judge those outside \add the church \add*? \add Are\add* you not to judge those inside? \v 13 God will judge those outside. “Expel the wicked \add man\add* from among you.”\f + \fr 5:13 \ft Literally \fqa Expel the evil from among you\ft ; \ref Deuteronomy 13:5|DEU 13:5\ref*, 17:7, 19:19, 21:21, 22:21, 22:24, and 24:7\f*
\c 6
\s1 Lawsuits among Believers
\p \v 1 \add If\add* any of you has a grievance against another, how dare he go to law before the unrighteous \add instead of\add* before the saints! \v 2 Do you not know that the saints will judge the world? And if you are to judge the world, are you not competent  \add to judge \add* trivial cases? \v 3 Do you not know that we will judge angels? How much more the things of this life!
\p \v 4 So if you need to settle everyday matters, do you appoint \add as judges\add* those of no standing in the church? \v 5 I say this to your shame. Is there really no one among you wise \add enough\add* to arbitrate between his brothers? \v 6 Instead, \add one\add* brother goes to law against \add another\add*, and this in front of unbelievers!
\p \v 7 The very fact that you have lawsuits among you means \add that\add* you \add are\add* thoroughly defeated already. Why not rather be wronged? Why not rather be cheated? \v 8 Instead, you yourselves cheat and do wrong, even \add against your own\add* brothers!\f + \fr 6:8 \ft Literally \fqa do wrong, and this to brothers!\f*
\s1 Members of Christ
\p \v 9 Do you not know that \add the\add* wicked will not inherit \add the\add* kingdom of God? \add Do\add* not be deceived: Neither the sexually immoral, nor idolaters, nor adulterers, nor men who submit to or perform homosexual acts, \v 10 nor thieves, nor the greedy, nor drunkards, nor verbal abusers, nor swindlers, will inherit \add the\add* kingdom of God. \v 11 And that \add is what\add* some \add of\add* you were. But you were washed, you were sanctified, you were justified, in the name of the Lord Jesus Christ and by the Spirit of our God.
\p \v 12 “Everything is permissible for me,” but not everything is beneficial. “Everything is permissible for me,” but I will not be mastered by anything. \v 13 “Food for the stomach and the stomach for food,” but God will destroy them both. The body \add is\add* not \add intended\add* for sexual immorality, but for the Lord, and the Lord for the body. \v 14 By His power God raised the Lord \add from the dead\add*, and He will raise us \add also\add*.
\p \v 15 Do you not know that your bodies are members of Christ? Shall I then take the members of Christ and unite \add them\add* with a prostitute? Never! \v 16 Or don’t you know that he who unites himself with a prostitute is one \add with her\add* in body? For it is said, “The two will become one flesh.”\f + \fr 6:16 \ref Genesis 2:24|GEN 2:24\ref* (see also LXX)\f* \v 17 But he who unites himself with the Lord is one \add with Him in\add* spirit.
\s1 The Temple of the Holy Spirit
\r (\ref Romans 12:1–8|ROM 12:1-8\ref*; \ref 1 Corinthians 3:16–23|1CO 3:16-23\ref*)
\p \v 18 Flee from sexual immorality. Every \add other\add* sin a man can commit is outside \add his\add* body, but he who sins sexually sins against \add his\add* own body. \v 19 Do you not know that your body \add is\add* a temple of the Holy Spirit \add who\add* is in you, whom you have \add received\add* from God? You are not your own; \v 20 you were bought at a price. Therefore glorify God with your body.\f + \fr 6:20 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa and with your spirit, which belong to God\ft .\f*
\c 7
\s1 Principles of Marriage
\p \v 1 Now for the matters you wrote about: \add It is\add* good to abstain from sexual relations.\f + \fr 7:1 \ft Literally \fqa It is good for a man not to touch a woman.\f* \v 2 But because \add there is so much\add* sexual immorality, each man should have his \add own\add* wife, and each woman \add her\add* own husband.
\p \v 3 The husband should fulfill \add his marital\add* duty to \add his\add* wife, and likewise the wife to \add her\add* husband. \v 4 The wife \add does\add* not have authority over \add her\add* own body, but the husband. Likewise the husband \add does\add* not have authority over \add his\add* own body, but the wife.
\p \v 5 \add Do\add* not deprive each other, except by mutual consent \add and\add* for a time, so you may devote yourselves to prayer. Then come together again, so that Satan will not tempt you through your lack of self-control. \v 6 I say this as a concession, not as a command. \v 7 I wish that all men were as I am. But each \add man\add* has his own gift from God; one \add has\add* this \add gift\add*, another \add has\add* that.
\p \v 8 Now to the unmarried and widows I say \add this\add*: \add It is\add* good for them to remain \add unmarried\add*, as I am. \v 9 But if they cannot control themselves, let them marry. For it is better to marry than to burn with passion.
\p \v 10 To the married I give this command (not I, but the Lord): A wife must not separate from \add her\add* husband. \v 11 But if she \add does\add*, she must remain unmarried or else be reconciled to \add her\add* husband. And a husband must not divorce \add his\add* wife.
\p \v 12 To the rest I say this (\add I,\add* not the Lord): If a brother has an unbelieving wife and she is willing to live with him, he must not divorce her. \v 13 And if a woman has an unbelieving husband and he is willing to live with her, she must not divorce \add him\add*. \v 14 For the unbelieving husband is sanctified through \add his believing\add* wife, and the unbelieving wife is sanctified through \add her believing\add* husband. Otherwise your children would be unclean, but now they are holy.
\p \v 15 But if the unbeliever leaves, let him go. The  \add believing \add* brother or sister is not bound in such \add cases\add*. God has called you\f + \fr 7:15 \ft SBL, BYZ, and TR \fqa us\f* \add to live\add* in peace. \v 16 How do you know, wife, whether you will save \add your\add* husband? Or how do you know, husband, whether you will save \add your\add* wife?
\s1 Live Your Calling
\p \v 17 Regardless, each one should lead  \add the life  that\add* the Lord has assigned \add to him and to which\add* God has called \add him\add*. This \add is what\add* I prescribe in all the churches. \v 18 \add Was\add* a man already circumcised when he was called? He should not become uncircumcised. \add Was\add* a man still uncircumcised when called? He should not be circumcised. \v 19 Circumcision is nothing and uncircumcision is nothing. Keeping God’s commandments \add is what counts\add*.
\p \v 20 Each \add one\add* should remain in the situation \add he was\add* in when he was called. \v 21 Were you a slave when you were called? Do not let it concern you— but if you can gain \add your\add* freedom, take the opportunity. \v 22 For he who \add was\add* a slave when he was called by \add the\add* Lord is \add the\add* Lord’s freedman. Conversely, he who \add was\add* a free man when he was called is Christ’s slave.
\p \v 23 You were bought at a price; \add do\add* not become slaves of men. \v 24 Brothers, each one should remain in the \add situation\add* he was in when God called him.
\s1 The Unmarried and Widowed
\p \v 25 Now about virgins, I have no command from \add the\add* Lord, but I give a judgment as \add one who\add* by \add the\add* Lord’s mercy is trustworthy. \v 26 Because of the present\f + \fr 7:26 \ft Or \fqa impending\f* crisis, I think \add it\add* is good for a man to remain as \add he is\add*. \v 27 Are you committed to a wife? \add Do\add* not seek to be released. Are you free of \add commitment\add*? \add Do\add* not look for a wife. \v 28 But if you do marry, you have not sinned. And if a virgin marries, she has not sinned. But \add those who marry\add* will face troubles in \add this\add* life, and I \add want to\add* spare you \add this\add*.
\p \v 29 \add What\add* I am saying, brothers, \add is\add* that the time is short. From now on those who have wives should live as if they had none; \v 30 those who weep, as if they \add did not\add*; those who are joyful, as if they were not; those who make a purchase, as if they had nothing; \v 31 and those who use the things of \add this\add* world, as if not dependent on them. For this world \add in its\add* present form is passing away.
\p \v 32 I want you to be free from concern. The unmarried man is concerned about the \add work\add* of the Lord, how he can please the Lord. \v 33 But the married man is concerned about the \add affairs\add* of this world, how he can please \add his\add* wife, \v 34 and \add his interests\add* are divided. The unmarried woman \add or\add* virgin is concerned about the \add work\add* of the Lord, how she can be holy in both body and spirit. But the married woman is concerned about the \add affairs\add* of this world, how she can please \add her\add* husband.
\p \v 35 I am saying this for your own good, not to restrict you, but in order to promote proper decorum and undivided devotion to the Lord.
\p \v 36 However, if someone thinks he is acting inappropriately toward his betrothed, \add and\add* if she is beyond \add her\add* youth and \add they\add* ought \add to marry\add*,\f + \fr 7:36 \ft Literally \fqa and it ought to be so\f* let him do as he wishes; he is not sinning; they should get married. \v 37 But the \add man who is\add* firmly established in his heart \add and\add* under no constraint, with control over \add his\add* will and resolve in \add his\add* heart \add not to marry\add* the virgin,\f + \fr 7:37 \ft Literally \fqa in his heart to keep the virgin \ft or \fqa in his heart to keep the betrothed\f* he will do well.
\p \v 38 So then, he who \add marries\add* the virgin does well, but he who \add does\add* not marry \add her\add* does even better.
\p \v 39 A wife is bound to her husband as long as he lives. But if \add her\add* husband dies, she is free to marry anyone she wishes, as long as \add he\add* belongs to \add the\add* Lord.\f + \fr 7:39 \ft Literally \fqa she is free to be married to whom she wishes, only in the Lord\f* \v 40 In my judgment, however, she is happier if she remains as she is. And I think that I too have \add the\add* Spirit of God.
\c 8
\s1 Food Sacrificed to Idols
\r (\ref Ezekiel 14:1–11|EZK 14:1-11\ref*; \ref Romans 14:13–23|ROM 14:13-23\ref*)
\p \v 1 Now about \add food\add* sacrificed to idols: We know that we all have knowledge. Knowledge puffs up, but love builds up. \v 2 The one who thinks he knows something does not yet know as he ought to know. \v 3 But the one who loves God is known by \add God\add*.
\p \v 4 So about eating food sacrificed to idols: We know that an idol \add is\add* nothing at all in \add the\add* world, and that \add there is\add* no God but one. \v 5 For even if there are so-called gods, whether in heaven or on earth (as there are many  \add so-called \add* gods and lords), \v 6 yet for us  \add there is but \add* one God, the Father, from whom all things \add came\add* and for \add whom\add* we \add exist\add*. And \add there is but \add* one Lord, Jesus Christ, through whom all things \add came\add* and through \add whom\add* we \add exist\add*.
\p \v 7 But not everyone \add has\add* this knowledge. Some \add people\add* are still so accustomed to idols \add that\add* they eat \add such food\add* as if it were sacrificed to an idol. And \add since\add* their conscience is weak, it is defiled. \v 8 But food does not bring us closer to God: We are no worse if we do not eat, \add and\add* no \add better\add* if we do.
\p \v 9 Be careful, however, \add that\add* your freedom does not become a stumbling block to the weak. \v 10 For if someone \add with\add* a weak conscience sees you who are well informed eating in an idol’s temple, \add will he\add* not be encouraged to eat \add food\add* sacrificed to idols? \v 11 \add So this\add* weak brother, for whom Christ died, is destroyed by your knowledge. \v 12 By sinning against \add your\add* brothers in this way and wounding their weak conscience, you sin against Christ.
\p \v 13 Therefore, if \add what I eat\add* causes my brother \add to stumble\add*, I will never eat meat \add again\add*, so that I will not cause \add him to stumble\add*.
\c 9
\s1 The Rights of an Apostle
\r (\ref Deuteronomy 18:1–8|DEU 18:1-8\ref*)
\p \v 1 Am I not free? Am I not an apostle? Have I not seen Jesus our Lord? Are you \add yourselves\add* not my workmanship in \add the\add* Lord? \v 2 Even if I am not an apostle to others, surely I am to you. For you are the seal of my apostleship in \add the\add* Lord.
\p \v 3 This is my defense to those who scrutinize me: \v 4 Have we no right to food and to drink? \v 5 Have we no right to take along a believing wife,\f + \fr 9:5 \ft Literally \fqa take along a sister—a wife—\f* as \add do\add* the other apostles and the Lord’s brothers and Cephas?\f + \fr 9:5 \ft That is, Peter\f* \v 6 Or \add are\add* Barnabas and I the only \add apostles \add* who must work for a living?\f + \fr 9:6 \ft Literally \fqa Or is it only Barnabas and I who do not have authority not to work?\f*
\p \v 7 Who serves as a soldier at his own expense? Who plants a vineyard and does not eat of its fruit? Who tends a flock and does not drink of \add its\add* milk?
\p \v 8 Do I say this from a human perspective? Doesn’t \add the\add* Law say the same thing? \v 9 For it is written in the Law of Moses: “Do not muzzle an ox \add while it is\add* treading out the grain.”\f + \fr 9:9 \ref Deuteronomy 25:4|DEU 25:4\ref*\f* \add Is it\add* about oxen \add that\add* God is concerned? \v 10 Isn’t He actually speaking on our behalf? Indeed, this was written for us, because \add when\add* the plowman plows and the thresher \add threshes \add*, \add they\add* should \add also\add* expect to share in the harvest.
\p \v 11 If we have sown spiritual \add seed\add* among you, \add is it\add* too much for us to reap a material \add harvest\add* from you? \v 12 If others have \add this\add* right to your \add support\add*, shouldn’t we \add have it\add* all the more? But we did not exercise this right. Instead, we put up with anything rather than hinder the gospel of Christ.
\p \v 13 Do you not know that those who work in the temple eat of \add its food\add*, \add and\add* those who serve at the altar partake of \add its offerings\add*? \v 14 In the same way, the Lord has prescribed that those who preach the gospel should receive their living from the gospel. \v 15 But I have not used \add any\add* of these \add rights\add*. And I am not writing this to suggest that something be done for me. Indeed, I would rather die than \add let\add* anyone nullify my boast.
\p \v 16 Yet when I preach the gospel, I have no reason to boast, because I am obligated \add to preach\add*. Woe to me if I do not preach the gospel! \v 17 If my \add preaching is\add* voluntary, I have a reward. But if \add it is\add* not voluntary, I am \add still\add* entrusted with \add a\add* responsibility. \v 18 What then is my reward? That in preaching the gospel I may offer \add it\add* free of charge, and so not use up my rights in \add preaching it\add*.
\s1 Paul the Servant to All
\p \v 19 Though I am free of obligation to anyone, I make myself a slave to everyone, to win as many as possible. \v 20 To the Jews I became like a Jew, to win \add the\add* Jews. To those under \add the\add* law \add I became like one\add* under \add the\add* law (\add though I\add* myself am not under \add the\add* law), to win those under \add the\add* law. \v 21 To those without \add the\add* law \add I became like one\add* without \add the\add* law (\add though I\add* am not outside \add the\add* law of God but \add am\add* under \add the\add* law of Christ), to win those without \add the\add* law. \v 22 To the weak I became weak, to win the weak. I have become all things to all \add people\add* so that by all possible means I might save some.
\p \v 23 I do all \add this\add* for the sake of the gospel, so that I may share in its \add blessings\add*.
\s1 Run Your Race to Win
\p \v 24 Do you not know that in a race all the runners run, but only one receives the prize? Run in such a way as to take \add the prize\add*. \v 25 Everyone who competes in the games trains with strict discipline. They \add do it for\add* a crown \add that is\add* perishable, but we \add do it for a crown  that is\add* imperishable. \v 26 Therefore I do not run aimlessly; I do not fight like I am beating \add the\add* air. \v 27 No, I discipline my body and make it my slave, \add so that\add* after I have preached to others, I myself will not be disqualified.
\c 10
\s1 Warnings from Israel’s Past
\r (\ref Numbers 16:41–50|NUM 16:41-50\ref*; \ref Numbers 25:1–5|NUM 25:1-5\ref*)
\p \v 1 I do not want you to be unaware, brothers, that our forefathers were all under the cloud, and \add that\add* they all passed through the sea. \v 2 They were all baptized into Moses in the cloud and in the sea. \v 3 They all ate the same spiritual food \v 4 and drank the same spiritual drink; for they drank from \add the\add* spiritual rock that accompanied them, and \add that\add* rock was Christ. \v 5 Nevertheless, God was not pleased with most of them, for they were struck down in the wilderness.
\p \v 6 These things took place as examples to keep us from craving evil things as they \add did\add*. \v 7 Do not be idolaters, as some of them \add were\add*. As it is written: “The people sat down to eat and drink and got up to indulge in revelry.”\f + \fr 10:7 \ft Or \fqa to play\ft ; \ref Exodus 32:6|EXO 32:6\ref*\f* \v 8 We should not commit sexual immorality, as some of them \add did\add*, and in one day twenty-three thousand \add of them\add* died. \v 9 We should not test Christ,\f + \fr 10:9 \ft WH, NE, and Tischendorf \fqa test the Lord\f* as some of them \add did\add*, and were killed by snakes. \v 10 And do not complain, as some of them \add did\add*, and were killed by the destroying angel.\f + \fr 10:10 \ft Literally \fqa the destroyer\f*
\p \v 11 Now these things happened to them \add as\add* examples and were written down as warnings for us, on whom the fulfillment of the ages has come. \v 12 So the \add one who\add* thinks he is standing firm should be careful not to fall. \v 13 No temptation has seized you except what is common to man. And God \add is\add* faithful; \add He\add* will not let you be tempted beyond what you can bear. But when \add you are\add* tempted, He will also provide an escape, so that you can stand up under \add it\add*.
\s1 Flee from Idolatry
\r (\ref Exodus 20:22–26|EXO 20:22-26\ref*)
\p \v 14 Therefore, my beloved, flee from idolatry. \v 15 I speak to reasonable \add people\add*; judge for yourselves what I say. \v 16 Is not the cup of blessing that we bless a participation in the blood of Christ? \add And\add* is not the bread that we break a participation in the body of Christ? \v 17 Because \add there is\add* one loaf, \add we who are\add* many are one body; for we all partake of the one loaf.
\p \v 18 Consider the people of Israel: Are not those who eat the sacrifices fellow partakers in the altar? \v 19 Am I suggesting, then, that food sacrificed to an idol is anything, or that an idol is anything? \v 20 No, \add but the\add* sacrifices of pagans are offered to demons, not to God. And I do not want you to be participants with demons. \v 21 You cannot drink \add the\add* cup of \add the\add* Lord and \add the\add* cup of demons \add too\add*; you cannot partake in \add the\add* table of \add the\add* Lord and \add the\add* table of demons \add too\add*. \v 22 Are we trying to provoke the Lord \add to jealousy\add*? Are we stronger than He?
\s1 All to God’s Glory
\r (\ref 1 Peter 4:1–11|1PE 4:1-11\ref*)
\p \v 23 “Everything is permissible,”\f + \fr 10:23 \ft Or \fqa “All things are lawful,” \ft twice in this verse\f* but not everything is beneficial. “Everything is permissible,” but not everything is edifying. \v 24 No one should seek his own \add good\add*, but \add the good\add* of others.
\p \v 25 Eat anything sold in \add the\add* meat market without raising questions of conscience, \v 26 for, “The earth \add is\add* the Lord’s, and the fullness thereof.”\f + \fr 10:26 \ref Psalm 24:1|PSA 24:1\ref*\f*
\p \v 27 If an unbeliever invites you \add to a meal\add* and you want to go, eat anything set before you without raising questions of conscience. \v 28 But if someone tells you, “This \add food\add* was offered to idols,” \add then do\add* not eat \add it\add*, for the sake of the one who told \add you\add* and for the sake of conscience —\f + \fr 10:28 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa and for the sake of conscience—for the earth is the Lord’s, and the fullness thereof—\f* \v 29 the other \add one’s conscience \add*, I mean, not your own. For why \add should\add* my freedom be determined by someone else’s conscience? \v 30 If I partake \add in the meal\add* with thankfulness, why am I denounced because of that for which I give thanks?
\p \v 31 So whether you eat or drink or whatever you do, do it all to the glory of God. \v 32 Do not become a stumbling block, whether to Jews \add or\add* Greeks \add or\add* the church of God— \v 33 as I also \add try to\add* please everyone in all \add I do\add*. \add For\add* I am not seeking my \add own\add* good, but \add the good\add* of many, that they may be saved.
\c 11
\s1 Roles in Worship
\p \v 1 You are to imitate me, just as I \add imitate\add* Christ.
\p \v 2 Now I commend you for remembering me in everything and \add for\add* maintaining the traditions, just as I passed \add them\add* on to you. \v 3 But I want you to understand that the head of every man is Christ, and \add the\add* head of \add the\add* woman \add is\add* man, and \add the\add* head of Christ \add is\add* God.
\p \v 4 Every man \add who\add* prays or prophesies with \add his\add* head covered dishonors his head. \v 5 And every woman \add who\add* prays or prophesies with \add her\add* head uncovered dishonors her head, for it is just \add as if\add* her head were shaved. \v 6 If a woman \add does\add* not cover her head, she should have her hair cut off. And if \add it is\add* shameful for a woman to have her hair cut or shaved off, she should cover her head.
\p \v 7 A man ought not to cover \add his\add* head, \add since\add* he is \add the\add* image and glory of God; but the woman is \add the\add* glory of man. \v 8 For man did not come from woman, but woman from man. \v 9 Neither \add was\add* man created for woman, but woman for man. \v 10 For this reason a woman ought to have a sign of authority on\f + \fr 11:10 \ft Or \fqa have authority over\f* \add her\add* head, because of the angels.
\p \v 11 In \add the\add* Lord, however, woman \add is\add* not independent \add of\add* man, nor \add is\add* man independent of woman. \v 12 For just as woman \add came\add* from man, so also man \add is\add* born of woman. But everything \add comes\add* from God.
\p \v 13 Judge for yourselves: Is it proper for a woman to pray to God with her head uncovered? \v 14 Doesn’t nature itself teach you that if a man has long hair, it is a disgrace to him, \v 15 but \add that\add* if a woman has long hair, it is her glory? For long hair is given to her as a covering. \v 16 If anyone is inclined to dispute \add this\add*, we have no \add other\add* practice, nor \add do\add* the churches of God.
\s1 Sharing in the Lord’s Supper
\r (\ref Matthew 26:20–30|MAT 26:20-30\ref*; \ref Mark 14:17–26|MRK 14:17-26\ref*; \ref Luke 22:14–23|LUK 22:14-23\ref*)
\p \v 17 In the \add following\add* instructions I have no praise to offer, because your gatherings do more harm than good. \v 18 First of all, I hear that \add when\add* you come together as a church, there are divisions among you, and in part I believe it. \v 19 And indeed, there must be differences among you to show which of you are approved.
\p \v 20 Now then, \add when\add* you come together, it is not \add the\add* Lord’s Supper you eat. \v 21 For as you eat, each \add of you\add* goes ahead without sharing his meal.\f + \fr 11:21 \ft Literally \fqa each one takes first his own meal.\f* While \add one\add* remains hungry, \add another\add* gets drunk. \v 22 Don’t you have \add your own\add* homes in which to eat and drink? Or do you despise the church of God and humiliate those who have nothing? What can I say to you? Shall I praise you for this? No, I will not!
\p \v 23 For I received from the Lord what I also passed on to you: The Lord Jesus, on the night He was betrayed, took bread, \v 24 and when He had given thanks, He broke \add it\add* and said, \wj “This is My body, which \add is\add* for you;\f + \fr 11:24 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa which is broken for you\f* do this in remembrance of Me.”\wj* \v 25 In the same way, after supper \add He took\add* the cup, saying, \wj “This cup is the new covenant in My blood; do this, as often as you drink \add it\add*, in remembrance of Me.”\wj* \v 26 For as often as you eat this bread and drink \add this\add* cup, you proclaim the Lord’s death until He comes.
\p \v 27 Therefore, whoever eats the bread or drinks the cup of the Lord in an unworthy manner will be guilty of sinning against the\f + \fr 11:27 \ft Or \fqa will be responsible for the\f* body and blood of the Lord. \v 28 Each one must examine himself \add before\add* he eats of the bread and drinks of the cup. \v 29 For anyone who eats and drinks without recognizing the body\f + \fr 11:29 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa the body of the Lord\f* eats and drinks judgment on himself. \v 30 That is why many among you \add are\add* weak and sick, and a number \add of you\add* have fallen asleep.
\p \v 31 Now if we judged ourselves \add properly\add*, we would not come under judgment. \v 32 But when we are judged by the Lord, we are being disciplined so that we will not be condemned with the world.
\p \v 33 So, my brothers, when you come together to eat, wait for one another. \v 34 If anyone is hungry, he should eat at home, so that when you come together \add it will not result\add* in judgment. And when I come, I will give instructions about the remaining matters.
\c 12
\s1 Spiritual Gifts
\p \v 1 Now about spiritual \add gifts\add*, brothers, I do not want you to be uninformed. \v 2 You know that when you were pagans, you were influenced \add and\add* led astray to mute idols. \v 3 Therefore I inform you that no one who is speaking by \add the\add* Spirit of God says, “Jesus \add be\add* cursed,” and no one can say, “Jesus \add is\add* Lord,” except by \add the\add* Holy Spirit.
\p \v 4 There are different gifts, but the same Spirit. \v 5 There are different ministries, but the same Lord. \v 6 There are different ways of working, but the same God works all things in all \add people\add*.
\p \v 7 Now to each one the manifestation of the Spirit is given for the common good. \v 8 To one there is given through the Spirit \add the\add* message of wisdom, to another \add the\add* message of knowledge by the same Spirit, \v 9 to another faith by the same Spirit, to another gifts of healing by that one Spirit, \v 10 to another \add the\add* working of miracles, to another prophecy, to another distinguishing between spirits, to another speaking in various tongues, and to \add still\add* another \add the\add* interpretation of tongues. \v 11 All these are the work of one and the same Spirit, who apportions \add them\add* to each one as He determines.
\s1 The Body of Christ
\p \v 12 The body is a unit, though it is composed of many parts. And \add although its\add* parts are many, they all form one body. So \add it is\add* with Christ. \v 13 For in one Spirit we were all baptized into one body, whether Jews or Greeks, slave or free, and we were all given one Spirit \add to drink\add*.
\p \v 14 For the body does not consist of one part, but of many. \v 15 If the foot should say, “Because I am not a hand, I do not belong to the body,” that would not  \add make it any less \add* a part of the body. \v 16 And if the ear should say, “Because I am not an eye, I do not belong to the body,” that would not  \add make it any less \add* a part of the body. \v 17 If the whole body \add were\add* an eye, where \add would\add* the sense of hearing \add be\add*? If \add the\add* whole \add body were\add* an ear, where \add would\add* the sense of smell \add be\add*?
\p \v 18 But in fact, God has arranged the members of the body, every one of them, according to His design. \v 19 If they were all one part, where \add would\add* the body \add be\add*? \v 20 As it is, \add there are\add* many parts, but one body.
\p \v 21 The eye cannot say to the hand, “I do not need you.” \add Nor can\add* the head \add say\add* to the feet, “I do not need you.” \v 22 On the contrary, the parts of the body that seem to be weaker are indispensable, \v 23 and the \add parts\add* we consider less honorable, we treat with greater honor. And our unpresentable \add parts\add* are treated with special modesty, \v 24 whereas our presentable \add parts\add* have no \add such\add* need.
\p But God has composed the body \add and\add* has given greater honor to the \add parts\add* that lacked it, \v 25 so that there should be no division in the body, but that \add its\add* members \add should have\add* mutual concern for one another. \v 26 If one part suffers, every part suffers with \add it\add*; if one part is honored, every part rejoices with \add it\add*.
\s1 The Greater Gifts
\p \v 27 Now you are \add the\add* body of Christ, and each of you \add is\add* a member \add of it\add*. \v 28 And in the church God has appointed first of all apostles, second prophets, third teachers, then \add workers of\add* miracles, \add and those with\add* gifts of healing, helping, administration, \add and\add* various tongues. \v 29 \add Are\add* all apostles? \add Are\add* all prophets? \add Are\add* all teachers? \add Do\add* all \add work\add* miracles? \v 30 \add Do\add* all have gifts of healing? \add Do\add* all speak in tongues? \add Do\add* all interpret? \v 31 But eagerly desire the greater gifts.
\p And now I will show you the most excellent way.
\c 13
\s1 Love
\p \v 1 If I speak in the tongues of men and of angels, but have not love, I am \add only a\add* ringing gong or \add a\add* clanging cymbal. \v 2 If I have the gift of prophecy and can fathom all mysteries and all knowledge, and if I have absolute faith so as to move mountains, but have not love, I am nothing. \v 3 If I give all I possess \add to the poor\add* and exult in the surrender of my body,\f + \fr 13:3 \ft SBL, NE, BYZ, and TR \fqa surrender my body to be burned\f* but have not love, I gain nothing.
\p \v 4 Love is patient, love is kind. \add It\add* does not envy, it does not boast, it is not proud. \v 5 It is not rude, it is not self-seeking, it is not easily angered, it keeps no account of wrongs. \v 6 \add Love\add* takes no pleasure in evil, but rejoices in the truth. \v 7 It bears all things, believes all things, hopes all things, endures all things.
\p \v 8 Love never fails. But where \add there are\add* prophecies, they will cease; where \add there are\add* tongues, they will be restrained; where \add there is\add* knowledge, it will be dismissed. \v 9 For we know in part and we prophesy in part, \v 10 but when the perfect comes, the partial passes away.
\p \v 11 When I was a child, I talked like a child, I thought like a child, I reasoned like a child. When I became a man, I set aside childish ways. \v 12 Now we see but a dim reflection as in a mirror; then \add we shall see\add* face to face. Now I know in part; then I shall know fully, even as I am fully known.
\p \v 13 And now these three remain: faith, hope, \add and\add* love; but \add the\add* greatest of these \add is\add* love.
\c 14
\s1 Prophecy and Tongues
\p \v 1 Earnestly pursue love and eagerly desire spiritual gifts, especially \add the gift\add* of prophecy. \v 2 For he who speaks in a tongue does not speak to men, but to God. Indeed, no one understands \add him\add*; he utters mysteries in the Spirit. \v 3 But he who prophesies speaks to men \add for their\add* edification, encouragement, and comfort. \v 4 The \add one who\add* speaks in a tongue edifies himself, but the \add one who\add* prophesies edifies \add the\add* church.
\p \v 5 I wish that all of you could speak in tongues, but \add I would\add* rather have you prophesy. He who prophesies \add is\add* greater than \add one who\add* speaks in tongues, unless he interprets so that the church may be edified.
\p \v 6 Now, brothers, if I come to you speaking in tongues, how will I benefit you, unless I \add bring\add* you \add some\add* revelation or knowledge or prophecy or teaching? \v 7 Even in the case of lifeless instruments, such as \add the\add* flute or harp, how will \add anyone\add* recognize \add the tune\add* they are playing unless the notes are distinct? \v 8 Again, if \add the\add* trumpet sounds a muffled call, who will prepare for battle? \v 9 So \add it is\add* with you. Unless you speak intelligible words with \add your\add* tongue, how will \add anyone\add* know what you are saying? You will \add just \add* be speaking into \add the\add* air.
\p \v 10 Assuredly, there are many different languages in \add the\add* world, yet none \add of them is\add* without meaning. \v 11 If, then, I do not know the meaning of someone’s language, I am a foreigner\f + \fr 14:11 \ft Literally \fqa a barbarian\ft ; twice in this verse\f* to the speaker, and \add he is\add* a foreigner to me.
\p \v 12 It is the same with you. Since you are eager to have spiritual gifts, strive to excel \add in gifts that\add* build up the church. \v 13 Therefore, the \add one who\add* speaks in a tongue should pray that he may interpret. \v 14 For if I pray in a tongue, my spirit prays, but my mind is unfruitful.
\p \v 15 What then \add shall I do\add*? I will pray with \add my\add* spirit, but I will also pray with \add my\add* mind. I will sing with \add my\add* spirit, but I will also sing with \add my\add* mind. \v 16 Otherwise, if you speak a blessing in spirit,\f + \fr 14:16 \ft Or \fqa in the Spirit\f* how \add can someone\add* who is uninstructed say “Amen” to your thanksgiving, since he does not know what you are saying? \v 17 You \add may\add* be giving thanks well enough, but the other one is not edified.
\p \v 18 I thank God that I speak in tongues more than all of you. \v 19 But in \add the\add* church, I would rather speak five coherent words to instruct others than ten thousand words in a tongue.
\p \v 20 Brothers, stop thinking like children. In \add regard to\add* evil be infants, but in \add your\add* thinking be mature. \v 21 It is written in the Law:
\b 
\q1 “By strange tongues and foreign lips
\q2 I will speak to this people,
\q1 \add but\add* even then they will \add not\add* listen to Me,
\q2 says \add the\add* Lord.”\f + \fr 14:21 \ref Isaiah 28:11–12|ISA 28:11-12\ref*\f*
\p \v 22 Tongues, then, are a sign, not for believers, but for unbelievers. Prophecy, however, \add is\add* for believers, not for unbelievers.
\p \v 23 So if the whole church comes together and everyone speaks in tongues, and some who are uninstructed or some unbelievers come in, will they not say that you are out of your minds? \v 24 But if an unbeliever or uninstructed person comes in while everyone is prophesying, he will be convicted \add and\add* called to account by all, \v 25 \add and\add* the secrets of his heart will be made known. So he will fall facedown \add and\add* worship God, proclaiming, “God is truly among you!”
\s1 Orderly Worship
\p \v 26 What then \add shall we say\add*, brothers? When you come together, everyone has a psalm \add or\add* a teaching, a revelation, a tongue, \add or\add* an interpretation. All \add of these\add* must be done to build up \add the church \add*.
\p \v 27 If anyone speaks in a tongue, two, or \add at\add* most three, \add should speak\add* in turn, and someone must interpret. \v 28 But if there is no interpreter, he should remain silent in \add the\add* church and speak \add only\add* to himself and God.
\p \v 29 Two or three prophets should speak, and the others should weigh carefully \add what is said\add*. \v 30 And if a revelation comes to someone who is seated, the first \add speaker\add* should stop. \v 31 For you can all prophesy in \add turn\add* so that everyone may be instructed and encouraged. \v 32 \add The\add* spirits of prophets are subject to prophets. \v 33 For \add God\add* is not a God of disorder, but of peace— as in all the churches of the saints.\f + \fr 14:33 \ft Or \fqa of peace. As in all the churches of the saints: \ft Thus, some translators begin the new paragraph after \fqa peace\ft .\f*
\p \v 34 Women are to be silent in the churches. They are not permitted to speak, but must be in submission, as the law says. \v 35 If they wish to inquire about something, they are to ask \add their\add* own husbands at home; for it is dishonorable for a woman to speak in \add the\add* church.\f + \fr 14:35 \ft Some manuscripts place verses 34–35 after verse 40.\f*
\p \v 36 \add Did\add* the word of God originate with you? Or \add are\add* you the only \add ones\add* it has reached? \v 37 If anyone considers \add himself\add* a prophet or spiritual person, let him acknowledge that what I am writing you is \add the\add* Lord’s command. \v 38 But if anyone ignores this, he himself will be ignored.\f + \fr 14:38 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa let him be ignorant\f*
\p \v 39 So, my brothers, be eager to prophesy, and \add do\add* not forbid speaking in tongues. \v 40 But everything must be done in a proper and orderly \add manner\add*.
\c 15
\s1 The Resurrection of Christ
\p \v 1 Now, brothers, I want to remind you \add of\add* the gospel I preached to you, which you received, and in which you stand firm. \v 2 By \add this gospel\add* you are saved, if you hold firmly to the word I preached to you. Otherwise, you have believed in vain.
\p \v 3 For what I received I passed on to you as of first importance: that Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures, \v 4 that He was buried, that He was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures, \v 5 and that He appeared to Cephas\f + \fr 15:5 \ft That is, Peter\f* \add and\add* then to the Twelve. \v 6 After that, He appeared to more than five hundred brothers at once, most of whom are still living, though some have fallen asleep. \v 7 Then He appeared to James, then to all the apostles. \v 8 And last of all He appeared to me also, as to \add one\add* of untimely birth.
\p \v 9 For I am the least of the apostles \add and\add* am unworthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the church of God. \v 10 But \add by the\add* grace of God I am what I am, and His grace to me was not in vain. No, I worked harder than all of them — yet not I, but the grace of God that \add was\add* with me. \v 11 Whether, then, \add it was\add* I or they, this is what we preach, and this is what you believed.
\s1 The Resurrection of the Dead
\p \v 12 But if it is preached that Christ has been raised from \add the\add* dead, how \add can\add* some of you say that there is no resurrection of \add the\add* dead? \v 13 If there is no resurrection of \add the\add* dead, \add then\add* not even Christ has been raised. \v 14 And if Christ has not been raised, our preaching \add is\add* worthless, and \add so is\add* your faith. \v 15 In that case, we are also exposed as false witnesses about God. For we have testified about God that He raised Christ \add from the dead\add*, \add but\add* He did not raise \add Him\add* if in fact \add the\add* dead are not raised.
\p \v 16 For if \add the\add* dead are not raised, then not even Christ has been raised. \v 17 And if Christ has not been raised, your faith is futile; you are still in your sins. \v 18 Then those also \add who\add* have fallen asleep in Christ have perished. \v 19 If our hope in Christ \add is\add* for this life alone, we are to be pitied more than all men.
\s1 The Order of Resurrection
\p \v 20 But Christ \add has\add* indeed been raised from \add the\add* dead, \add the\add* firstfruits of those who have fallen asleep. \v 21 For since death \add came\add* through a man, \add the\add* resurrection of \add the\add* dead \add comes\add* also through a man. \v 22 For as in Adam all die, so in Christ all will be made alive. \v 23 But each in \add his\add* own turn: Christ \add the\add* firstfruits; then at His coming, those who belong to \add Him\add*.
\p \v 24 Then the end \add will come\add*, when He hands over the kingdom to God \add the\add* Father after He has destroyed all dominion, authority, and power. \v 25 For He must reign until He has put all \add His\add* enemies under His feet. \v 26 \add The\add* last enemy to be destroyed \add is\add* death. \v 27 For “\add God\add* has put everything under His feet.”\f + \fr 15:27 \ref Psalm 8:6|PSA 8:6\ref*\f* Now when it says that everything has been put under Him, this clearly \add does\add* not include the \add One who\add* put everything under Him. \v 28 And when all things have been subjected to Him, then the Son Himself will be made subject to Him who put all things \add under\add* Him, so that God may be all in all.
\p \v 29 If \add these things are\add* not so, what will those do who are baptized for the dead? If \add the\add* dead are not raised at all, why are \add people\add* baptized for them? \v 30 And why \add do\add* we endanger ourselves every hour? \v 31 I face death every day, brothers, as surely as I boast about you in Christ Jesus our Lord. \v 32 If I fought wild beasts in Ephesus for human \add motives\add*, what did I gain? If \add the\add* dead are not raised,
\b 
\q1 “Let us eat and drink,
\q2 for tomorrow we die.”\f + \fr 15:32 \ref Isaiah 22:13|ISA 22:13\ref*\f*
\p \v 33 \add Do\add* not be deceived: “Bad company corrupts good character.”\f + \fr 15:33 \ft Probably a quote from the Greek comedy \fqa Thais \ft by Menander\f* \v 34 Sober up as you ought, and stop sinning; for some  \add of you \add* are ignorant of God. I say this to your shame.
\s1 The Resurrection Body
\p \v 35 But someone will ask, “How \add are\add* the dead raised? With what kind of body will they come?” \v 36 You fool! What you sow does not come to life unless it dies. \v 37 And what you sow \add is\add* not the body that will be, but \add just\add* a seed, perhaps of wheat or something else. \v 38 But God gives it a body as He has designed, and to each \add kind\add* of seed \add He gives\add* its own body.
\p \v 39 Not all flesh \add is\add* the same: Men \add have\add* one \add kind of\add* flesh, animals \add have\add* another, birds another, and fish another. \v 40 \add There are\add* also heavenly bodies and earthly bodies. But the splendor of the heavenly \add bodies is of\add* one \add degree\add*, and \add the splendor\add* of the earthly \add bodies is of\add* another. \v 41 \add The\add* sun \add has\add* one \add degree of\add* splendor, \add the\add* moon another, and \add the\add* stars another; and star differs from star in splendor.
\p \v 42 So \add will it be\add* with the resurrection of the dead: What is sown \add is\add* perishable; it is raised imperishable. \v 43 It is sown in dishonor; it is raised in glory. It is sown in weakness; it is raised in power. \v 44 It is sown a natural body; it is raised a spiritual body. If there is a natural body, there is also a spiritual \add body\add*. \v 45 So it is written: “The first man Adam became a living being”;\f + \fr 15:45 \ref Genesis 2:7|GEN 2:7\ref*\f* the last Adam a life-giving spirit.
\p \v 46 The spiritual, however, \add was\add* not first, but the natural, and then the spiritual. \v 47 The first man \add was\add* of the dust of \add the\add* earth, the second man from heaven. \v 48 As \add was\add* the earthly \add man\add*, so also \add are\add* those who are of the earth; and as \add is\add* the heavenly \add man\add*, so also \add are\add* those who are of heaven. \v 49 And just as we have borne the likeness of the earthly \add man\add*, so also shall we bear the likeness of the heavenly \add man\add*.
\s1 Where, O Death, Is Your Victory?
\r (\ref Hosea 13:9–14|HOS 13:9-14\ref*)
\p \v 50 Now I declare \add to you\add*, brothers, that flesh and blood cannot inherit \add the\add* kingdom of God, nor \add does\add* the perishable inherit the imperishable.
\p \v 51 Listen, I tell you a mystery: \add We will\add* not all sleep, but we will all be changed — \v 52 in an instant, in \add the\add* twinkling of an eye, at the last trumpet. For the trumpet will sound, the dead will be raised imperishable, and we will be changed. \v 53 For the perishable must be clothed\f + \fr 15:53 \ft Or \fqa clothe itself\f* with \add the\add* imperishable, and the mortal \add with\add* immortality.
\p \v 54 When the perishable has been clothed with the imperishable and the mortal \add with\add* immortality,\f + \fr 15:54 \ft WH does not include \fqa and the mortal with immortality\ft .\f* then the saying that is written will come to pass: “Death has been swallowed up in victory.”\f + \fr 15:54 \ref Isaiah 25:8|ISA 25:8\ref*\f*
\b 
\q1 \v 55 “Where, O Death, \add is\add* your victory?
\q2 Where, O Death, \add is\add* your sting?”\f + \fr 15:55 \ref Hosea 13:14|HOS 13:14\ref* (see also LXX); BYZ and TR \fqa “Where, O Death, is your sting? Where, O Hades, is your victory?”\f*
\p \v 56 The sting of death \add is\add* sin, and the power of sin \add is\add* the law. \v 57 But thanks \add be\add* to God, who gives us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ!
\p \v 58 Therefore, my beloved brothers, be steadfast \add and\add* immovable. Always excel in the work of the Lord, because you know that your labor in \add the\add* Lord is not in vain.
\c 16
\s1 The Collection for the Saints
\r (\ref 2 Corinthians 9:1–15|2CO 9:1-15\ref*)
\p \v 1 Now about the collection for the saints, you are to do as I directed the churches of Galatia: \v 2 On the first \add day\add* of every week, each of you should set aside a portion of his income, saving it up, so that when I come no collections will be needed. \v 3 Then, on my arrival, I will send letters with those you recommend to carry your gift to Jerusalem. \v 4 And if it is advisable for me to go \add also\add*, they can travel with me.
\s1 Paul’s Travel Plans
\r (\ref Romans 15:23–33|ROM 15:23-33\ref*)
\p \v 5 After I go through Macedonia, however, I will come to you; for I will be going through Macedonia. \v 6 Perhaps I will stay with you \add awhile\add*, or even spend the winter, so that you can help me \add on my journey\add*, wherever I go. \v 7 For I do not want to see you now \add only\add* in passing; I hope to spend some time with you, if the Lord permits. \v 8 But I will stay in Ephesus until Pentecost,\f + \fr 16:8 \ft That is, Shavuot, the late spring feast of pilgrimage to Jerusalem; it is also known as \fqa the Feast of Harvest\ft (see \ref Exodus 23:16|EXO 23:16\ref*) or \fqa the Feast of Weeks\ft (see \ref Exodus 34:22|EXO 34:22\ref*).\f* \v 9 because a great door \add for\add* effective \add work\add* has opened to me, even though many oppose \add me\add*.
\s1 Timothy and Apollos
\r (\ref Philippians 2:19–30|PHP 2:19-30\ref*)
\p \v 10 If Timothy comes, see to it that he has nothing to fear \add while he is\add* with you, for he is doing the work of \add the\add* Lord, just as I am. \v 11 No one, then, should treat him with contempt. Send him \add on his way\add* in peace so that he can return to me, for I am expecting him along with the brothers.
\p \v 12 Now about \add our\add* brother Apollos: I strongly urged him to go to you with the brothers. He was not at all inclined to go now, but he will go when he has the opportunity.
\s1 Concluding Exhortations
\p \v 13 Be on the alert. Stand firm in the faith. Be men of courage. Be strong. \v 14 Do everything in love.
\p \v 15 You know that Stephanas \add and his\add* household were \add the\add* first converts in Achaia, and they have devoted themselves to the service of the saints. Now I urge you, brothers, \v 16 to submit to such as these, and to every fellow worker and laborer.
\p \v 17 I am glad that Stephanas, Fortunatus, and Achaicus have arrived, because they have supplied what was lacking from you. \v 18 For they refreshed my spirit and yours \add as well\add*. Show your appreciation, therefore, to such men.
\s1 Signature and Final Greetings
\r (\ref Colossians 4:15–18|COL 4:15-18\ref*; \ref 2 Thessalonians 3:16–18|2TH 3:16-18\ref*)
\p \v 19 The churches in \add the province of\add* Asia\f + \fr 16:19 \ft Literally \fqa in Asia\ft ; Asia was a Roman province in what is now western Turkey. \f* send you greetings.
\p Aquila and Prisca\f + \fr 16:19 \fqa Prisca \ft is a variant of \fqa Priscilla\ft ; see \ref Acts 18:2|ACT 18:2\ref*.\f* greet you warmly in \add the\add* Lord, and so does the church \add that meets\add* at their house.
\p \v 20 All the brothers \add here\add* send you greetings. Greet one another with a holy kiss.
\p \v 21 This greeting \add is in my\add* own hand — Paul.
\p \v 22 If anyone \add does\add* not love the Lord, let him be under a curse. Come, O Lord!\f + \fr 16:22 \ft Greek \fqa Marana Tha! \ft from a transliteration of the Aramaic, an exclamation of approaching divine judgment\f*
\p \v 23 The grace of the Lord Jesus \add be\add* with you.
\p \v 24 My love \add be\add* with all of you in Christ Jesus.
\p Amen.\f + \fr 16:24 \ft SBL, WH, and NA do not include \fqa Amen.\f*
